---
import PhilosophyLayout from "../layouts/PhilosophyLayout.astro";

const chapters = [
  {
    volume: "Volume I: Latent Form Theory",
    id: "volume-1",
    sections: [
      { title: "Overview", id: "v1-overview" },
      { title: "1. The Blank Canvas", id: "v1-chapter-1" },
      { title: "2. Entropic Reduction", id: "v1-chapter-2" },
      { title: "3. Reading Material", id: "v1-chapter-3" },
      { title: "4. Axis of Resolution", id: "v1-chapter-4" },
    ],
  },
  {
    volume: "Volume II: Modern Stratigraphy",
    id: "volume-2",
    sections: [
      { title: "Overview", id: "v2-overview" },
      { title: "1. Matter Before Image", id: "v2-chapter-1" },
      { title: "2. Deep Time", id: "v2-chapter-2" },
      { title: "3. Selection as Composition", id: "v2-chapter-3" },
      { title: "4. Philosophy of Context", id: "v2-chapter-4" },
      { title: "5. Material Honesty", id: "v2-chapter-5" },
      { title: "6. Nexus of Timescales", id: "v2-chapter-6" },
      { title: "7. Coordinate and Canvas", id: "v2-chapter-7" },
    ],
  },
  {
    volume: "Volume III: The Disciplined Eye",
    id: "volume-3",
    sections: [
      { title: "Preface", id: "v3-preface" },
      { title: "1. Perception", id: "v3-part-1" },
      { title: "2. Taste", id: "v3-part-2" },
      { title: "3. Constraint", id: "v3-part-3" },
      { title: "4. Silence", id: "v3-part-4" },
      { title: "5. Stewardship", id: "v3-part-5" },
      { title: "6. The Maker", id: "v3-final" },
    ],
  },
];
---

<PhilosophyLayout title="Latent Form Theory">
  <div class="theory-layout">
    <aside class="theory-sidebar">
      <h2>Latent Form Theory</h2>
      {
        chapters.map((vol) => (
          <div class="sidebar-volume">
            <a href={`#${vol.id}`}>{vol.volume}</a>
            <ul>
              {vol.sections.map((sec) => (
                <li>
                  <a href={`#${sec.id}`}>{sec.title}</a>
                </li>
              ))}
            </ul>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </aside>

    <main class="theory-content">
      <!-- INTRO -->
      <section id="intro">
        <h1>Latent Form Theory</h1>
        <p class="lead">
          Every creative work begins with abundance: countless possible forms, directions, and outcomes. Through disciplined choice, the maker removes what is unnecessary until the remaining form achieves coherence, balance, and necessity.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- VOLUME 1 -->
      <section id="volume-1">
        <h2>Volume I: Latent Form Theory</h2>

        <h3 id="v1-overview">The Philosophy of Essential Subtraction</h3>
        <p class="lead">
          Latent Form Theory: The Revelation of Form Through Essential Subtraction
        </p>

        <h3>Introduction: The Form That Was Already Waiting</h3>
        <p>
          I was eleven years old when I first encountered an idea that would quietly follow me for the rest of my life.
        </p>
        <p>
          My family was backpacking through Kentucky along the Daniel Boone Trail when we stopped at an old country store in Berea. Outside sat an old man with a pocket knife in his hand and a small piece of wood resting against his palm.
        </p>

        <h3 id="v1-chapter-1">Chapter 1 — The Blank Canvas</h3>
        <h4 id="v1-ch1-p1">I. The Myth of the Blank Canvas</h4>
        <p>
          A common belief in design is that creation begins with emptiness. The artist approaches a blank canvas. The sculptor approaches an untouched block of stone.
        </p>

        <h4 id="v1-ch1-p2">II. The Material That Already Knows</h4>
        <p>
          Industrial processes often depend upon reducing variation. A standardized board is valuable because it behaves predictably.
        </p>

        <h4 id="v1-ch1-p3">III. Two Ways of Approaching Material</h4>
        <p>
          There are two fundamentally different relationships a maker can have with material: imposition and discovery.
        </p>

        <h4 id="v1-ch1-p4">IV. Reading What Already Exists</h4>
        <p>
          To work through subtraction requires a different kind of attention. The surface of the material becomes evidence.
        </p>

        <h4 id="v1-ch1-p5">V. The First Reduction</h4>
        <p>
          The first removal of material is often misunderstood as the beginning of creation. In reality, it is the beginning of clarification.
        </p>

        <h4 id="v1-ch1-p6">VI. The Emergence of Inevitability</h4>
        <p>
          The most successful works often possess a peculiar quality: they seem as though they could not have been otherwise.
        </p>

        <h3 id="v1-chapter-2">Chapter 2 — Entropic Reduction</h3>
        <h4 id="v1-ch2-p1">I. The Movement Toward Disorder</h4>
        <p>
          Every material exists within a constant state of change. Before form becomes clear, possibility exists everywhere.
        </p>

        <h4 id="v1-ch2-p2">II. Reduction as Direction</h4>
        <p>
          Subtraction is often misunderstood as a purely negative action. Every removal is also a direction.
        </p>

        <h3 id="v1-chapter-3">Chapter 3 — Reading Material as Structure</h3>
        <p>
          Before the hand shapes the material, the mind must learn to see it. The maker recognizes the order already present.
        </p>

        <h3 id="v1-chapter-4">Chapter 4 — The Axis of Resolution</h3>
        <p>
          The point of alignment where the behavior of the material, the geometry of the form, and the decisions of the maker agree.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- VOLUME 2 -->
      <section id="volume-2">
        <h2>Volume II: Modern Stratigraphy</h2>

        <h3 id="v2-overview">Overview: Material Memory and the Organization of Earth</h3>
        <p class="lead">
          Matter is not inert. It is intent, compressed by time, waiting for a hand patient enough to read it.
        </p>

        <h3 id="v2-chapter-1">Chapter 1 — Matter Before Image</h3>
        <p>
          Matter precedes the image. Long before a single mark is made, the physical properties of the medium have already dictated what that mark can legally be.
        </p>

        <h3 id="v2-chapter-2">Chapter 2 — Deep Time and Material Memory</h3>
        <p>
          Materials are not simply objects in space; they are structures made of time. Every object inherits that temporal depth whether the maker acknowledges it or not.
        </p>

        <h3 id="v2-chapter-3">Chapter 3 — Selection as Composition</h3>
        <p>
          Choosing one board over another is not preparation for the compositional act—it IS the compositional act. Selection is an act of recognition.
        </p>

        <h3 id="v2-chapter-4">Chapter 4 — Philosophy of Context</h3>
        <p>
          A mineral resting undisturbed in a local clay seam is geology. Bind it to a panel within a precise boundary, and it becomes art.
        </p>

        <h3 id="v2-chapter-5">Chapter 5 — Authenticity and Material Honesty</h3>
        <p>
          An ethical commitment to material honesty—never disguising what a material actually is, and preserving its history.
        </p>

        <h3 id="v2-chapter-6">Chapter 6 — Nexus of Timescales</h3>
        <p>
          The finished object becomes a meeting point where biological and geological timescales converge with human observation.
        </p>

        <h3 id="v2-chapter-7">Chapter 7 — Coordinate and Canvas</h3>
        <p>
          Mapping spatial coordinates and material origin directly into the canvas structure.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- VOLUME 3 -->
      <section id="volume-3">
        <h2>Volume III: The Disciplined Eye</h2>

        <h3 id="v3-preface">Preface — Perception, Judgment, and the Ethics of Seeing</h3>
        <p class="lead">
          This volume is not about design. It is about the observer.
        </p>

        <h3 id="v3-part-1">Part I — Perception</h3>
        <p>
          Perception is not a passive intake of light. It is an active discipline of attention, shaped by experience and reflection.
        </p>

        <h3 id="v3-part-2">Part II — Taste</h3>
        <p>
          Taste is not preference. Taste is the cultivated ability to distinguish between decisions that reveal understanding and decisions that merely imitate it.
        </p>

        <h3 id="v3-part-3">Part III — Constraint</h3>
        <p>
          Constraint is the condition that makes originality possible, not an obstacle to it.
        </p>

        <h3 id="v3-part-4">Part IV — Silence</h3>
        <p>
          Absence as an active decision in composition. A void creates tension; a gap creates rhythm.
        </p>

        <h3 id="v3-part-5">Part V — Stewardship</h3>
        <p>
          The maker as temporary custodian of a tradition, not its heroic originator.
        </p>

        <h3 id="v3-final">Final Chapter — The Maker</h3>
        <p>
          The Maker no longer asks "what can I add?" before first asking "what is already here?"
        </p>
      </section>
    </main>
  </div>
</PhilosophyLayout>
<style>
.theory-layout {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 280px 1fr;
  gap: 80px;
  max-width: 1200px;
  margin: 0 auto;
}

.theory-sidebar {
  position: relative;
}

.theory-sidebar {
  position: sticky;
  top: 40px;
  height: fit-content;
}

.theory-sidebar h2 {
  font-family: Georgia, serif;
  font-size: 1.5rem;
  margin-bottom: 30px;
}

.sidebar-volume {
  margin-bottom: 30px;
}

.sidebar-volume > a {
  font-weight: 600;
  text-decoration: none;
  color: var(--text);
}

.sidebar-volume ul {
  list-style: none;
  padding-left: 0;
  margin-top: 12px;
}

.sidebar-volume li {
  margin-bottom: 8px;
}

.sidebar-volume li a {
  text-decoration: none;
  color: var(--muted);
  font-size: .9rem;
}


.theory-content {
  max-width: 720px;
}


@media (max-width:900px){

.theory-layout {
  display:block;
}

.theory-sidebar {
  position:relative;
  top:auto;
  margin-bottom:40px;
}

}

</style>